WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.510
- One of the most common
problems I hear about nowadays.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.360
And I'm sure everyone else does

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.280
is this feeling of being chronically tired

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.540
Because sometimes it feels like

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.680
no matter how much sleep you get

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.900
you just can't seem to perk up,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.520
feel energetic for most of
the day, every single day,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.160
so that you can tackle your
day and your life with energy.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.580
You know, this lack of
energy throughout the day,

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.520
this chronic tiredness is something

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.140
that you would expect to happen to you

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.040
if you were like 60 years
old, 70, 80 years old

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.980
but it's happening to 20 and
30 year olds, 15 year olds

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.440
young people who are supposed

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.650
to have this youthful exuberance to them,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.950
but they find themselves operating in life

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.370
with a sluggishness and dread.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.780
Believe me, I've been there.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.890
I've been there so many times in my life.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:47.350
So many times in fact,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.350
that it really caused me
to dive deep and figure out

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.000
what are the things that are
actually going to contribute

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.420
to me having more energy

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.070
and how do I make those things
happen every single day.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.460
And through all my research
and self experimentation

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.230
I've found out that the
most important things

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.550
are kind of the things
that you hear all the time.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.650
Sleep, exercise, and diet.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.920
You might be tempted now to
click away from this video

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.440
because you already know this

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.040
but that's exactly where
I ran into problems.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.320
I was treating sleep, exercise
and diet like a checklist.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.150
It's like, yeah, I get a
reasonable amount of sleep.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:21.010
I exercise.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.230
I'm an athletic guy.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.600
Yeah, I eat food.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.570
So those things can't be the reason

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:26.740
why I'm feeling sluggish.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.130
So I started looking for
these like quick tips

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.000
and maybe I was iron deficient

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.140
or maybe I had a gluten sensitivity

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.010
or maybe I needed to
decalcify my pineal gland.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.430
I skipped over the fundamentals in search

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.900
for some miracle pill that
would make me less tired

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.733
throughout the day.

00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:44.540
It wasn't until I revisited the basics

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.090
and try to absolutely nail them

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.800
that I realized that was
the problem the whole time.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.000
Especially sleep.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.060
So in this video

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.330
I wanna go over some things
that I've implemented

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.350
in my life that really has
given me a lot more energy

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.500
throughout the day on a consistent basis.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.510
And yeah, hopefully they
work for you as well.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.420
- [Narrator] Are you always tired?

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.840
Do you move around the house?

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.170
Dreaming big dreams

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.800
but like the energy to
make them come true?

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.784
Well, maybe it's because you suck.

00:02:12.784 --> 00:02:13.670
(bright upbeat music)

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:14.503
What the fuck?

00:02:14.503 --> 00:02:17.950
- Okay, so thing number one,
I stopped misusing caffeine.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.010
Now caffeine is one of those weird ones.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.030
People think they know a lot
about how caffeine works.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:24.210
You know, you've probably heard

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.210
that caffeine lasts between
five and seven hours.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.280
So, you know, don't
drink too much caffeine

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.420
in the afternoon, or you might
not be able to fall asleep.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.270
But the weird thing about caffeine is

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.510
that it lasts a lot longer
than five to seven hours.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.630
The half-life of caffeine
is five to seven hours.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.740
And half-life means that
after five to seven hours

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.880
half the caffeine is still in your system.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.550
And then it takes another
five to seven hours after that

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.350
for half of that amount of
caffeine to leave your system.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.320
So if you have a cup of
coffee at like 4:00 PM,

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:54.830
which is, you know kind of late,

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.660
but it's probably not
gonna ruin your sleep

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.190
or something like that.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.540
Think again, if you have coffee at 4:00 PM

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.630
by 11:00 PM, there's a good chance

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:05.110
that 50% of that caffeine
is still in your system.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.470
That means by like 5:00 AM.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:08.380
There's a good chance

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.770
that a quarter of the
original 4:00 PM cup of coffee

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.040
is still in your system.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.210
You know, deep into the night

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.760
is when you're supposed to be
having some of your deepest,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.950
most restorative sleep of your life.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.720
This is the kind of sleep
that helps you form memories

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.050
process the information
you were bombarded with

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.370
throughout the day, form connections

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.110
form new ideas, so that
you can be more creative.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.150
The kind of sleep that's
supposed to be healing

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.040
your muscle tissues, purging your body

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.710
of adenosine so that you don't
feel tired in the morning.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:38.390
There's a lot of important
things that are going on

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.970
while you're sleeping and
your body being filled

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.460
with this caffeine is seriously
inhibiting those processes.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.210
So the simple tweak that I did

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.780
that helped me not misuse caffeine

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.050
is to just not drink
caffeine past lunchtime.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.400
I have the one that I wake
up with in the morning.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.350
And since I'm a little
bit of a caffeine fiend,

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.730
I have another one about
two hours after that

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.860
to really get my morning productivity

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.660
the kind of meth head
high that I'm looking for.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.760
I'll still get the caffeine
crash late after lunch.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.140
I expect that, it's the
kind of game you play.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.100
If you're a caffeine addict like me,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.360
but at least when I want to
put my head on the pillow

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.160
after a hard day's work

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.940
I'm actually able to get the
sleep that my body needs.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.240
So do not drink caffeine past 12:00 PM.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:22.160
Just don't do it.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.130
The other thing that
I started implementing

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.390
was I gave myself a lot
more sleep opportunity.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.740
Everyone's heard that you need
eight hours of sleep a night,

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.440
but the truth is in order
to sleep for that long,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.900
you probably need to be in bed for longer.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.300
I take about half an hour to 45 minutes

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.140
to fall asleep every single night.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.520
So I need to be in bed half
an hour or 45 minutes earlier

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.160
than I'm planning on falling asleep.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.120
And that makes sure

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.570
that I actually get the sleep

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:47.940
that I need every single night.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.640
Because the benefits
of a good night's sleep

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.210
is something that's cumulative.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.320
You know, if you give
yourself that much opportunity

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.770
every single night, your
amount of sleep baseline

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.810
will go up over a long period of time.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.840
And you'll kind of work
off your sleep debt.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.690
You'll work off your sleep deprivation

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.900
that you've been chronically plagued with.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.330
All you need to do is give yourself

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.050
more opportunity to sleep.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:10.530
The other thing that I did was
I fixed my circadian rhythm.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.540
For those of you who don't know

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.380
your circadian rhythm has sort
of like this internal clock

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.880
that tells your brain whether
it's daytime or nighttime.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.310
When you should be given energy,

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:21.540
and when you should slow
down and get ready for bed.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:25.270
And a lot of your circadian
rhythm is dictated by sunlight.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:28.510
How bright it is around you,
how dark it is around you.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:29.730
Which is why you've heard

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.880
that you shouldn't go on
social media or on smartphones,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.720
or look at screens right before bed,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.670
which is very, very true.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:38.430
When you're looking at
all this blue light,

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.620
your brain thinks that it's daytime.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.480
So it's not giving you the
melatonin that you need

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:44.820
that your brain needs to know

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.410
that it's like time to slow
down and go to bed now.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:48.970
So one thing that's really helped me

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:52.080
is to not look at screens
right before I go to bed.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.630
And especially not in bed.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.060
That way my brain can actually

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.630
start producing adequate melatonin.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.090
You know, I can start
slowing down psychologically.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.970
And by the time my head hits the pillow

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.757
during my little sleep opportunity window,

00:06:04.757 --> 00:06:06.120
I'm out like a light.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.800
The other thing I did to
fix my circadian rhythms

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.320
was I pinned a black
blanket over my windows

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.490
because I'm a barbarian with
no sense of interior design.

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.760
But what that did was

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.020
when it's the earlier hours of the morning

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.740
when I'm still sleeping, like
5:00 AM, 6:00 AM, 7:00 AM.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.150
I still want it to be dark

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.000
so I can still be deep in sleep.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.600
Getting as much high
quality sleep as I can.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.020
Rather than having all this
sun blasting in the window

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.010
interrupting my hard earned slumber.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.570
So make your room darker so
that for the time you're asleep

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.060
your brain thinks it's nighttime

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.380
and that you should be sleeping.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.710
And the last thing that I
implemented to improve my sleep

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.250
seems like a little quick fix.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:44.870
I don't know if this applies to you.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.050
But I was reading this book
called Breath by James Nester.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.240
And he talked about the
benefits of nasal breathing

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.010
as opposed to mouth breathing.

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.590
And I noticed that when I
put my head on my pillow

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:57.220
every night, my nose
gets really congested.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.700
And I end up opting in
the middle of the night

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:00.970
to mouth breathing.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.210
And in the book he talks
about how nasal breathing

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.640
is a lot better for you.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.340
So anyways, if you can't breathe
out of your nose at night

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.600
fix that, I fixed it by
using Breathe Right strips.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.800
This video is not
sponsored by Breathe Right,

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.090
but that's just what I did.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.120
And they work for me.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.350
I'll put a Amazon,

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:17.500
actually, I'm not going to put an Amazon,

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.030
like just type in Breathe Right strips.

